Here are the three C# 13 breaking changes and their fixes:

## 1. `Buffer.cs` — `[InlineArray]` on `record struct` is now illegal

C# 13 prohibits `[InlineArray]` on `record struct` because the compiler-synthesized members (equality, `ToString`, etc.) conflict with inline array layout. **Fix: use a plain `struct`.**

```csharp
[System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InlineArray(10)]
struct Buffer
{
    private int _element0;
}
```

## 2. `Generator.cs` — `stackalloc` in iterator local function

C# 13 relaxed rules to allow `unsafe` code in iterators/async methods, but as a side-effect, the compiler now **analyzes** the local function in the iterator context and rejects `stackalloc` (it can't live on a state-machine–captured frame). Previously the compiler didn't look inside. **Fix: extract to a regular `static` method.**

```csharp
unsafe class DataGenerator
{
    IEnumerable<int> Generate()
    {
        yield return 1;
        Process();
    }

    static void Process()
    {
        int* p = stackalloc int[10];
        // ... work with pointer
    }
}
```

## 3. `Resolver.cs` — ambiguous overload with `[]`

C# 13 added `params ReadOnlySpan<T>` / `params Span<T>` support, changing overload resolution. The empty collection `[]` now matches **both** `ReadOnlySpan<int>` and `Span<object>`, making the call ambiguous. **Fix: disambiguate with an explicit type.**

```csharp
class Resolver
{
    static void Process(ReadOnlySpan<int> ros) {}
    static void Process(Span<object> s) {}

    static void Main()
    {
        Process(ReadOnlySpan<int>.Empty);
    }
}
```

**Summary:**

| File | Breaking change | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `Buffer.cs` | `InlineArray` banned on `record struct` | Change to `struct` |
| `Generator.cs` | `stackalloc` now seen inside iterator local functions | Extract to a standalone `static` method |
| `Resolver.cs` | `[]` ambiguous across span overloads | Use an explicit type like `ReadOnlySpan<int>.Empty` |
